WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.090
SAAD ALI: Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:02.190
My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:05.340
I am an engineer on
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.240
and I'm joined here
by Aparna Sinha.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.230
She is the group
program manager--

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.530
product manager for all of
Kubernetes and our Google

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.922
Kubernetes Engine.

00:00:14.922 --> 00:00:16.130
Pleasure to have you, Aparna.

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:17.100
APARNA SINHA: Thank you, Saad.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:17.933
Pleasure to be here.

00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:20.220
SAAD ALI: So you have
been deeply involved

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:25.440
with the strategy for GKE and
defining the product vision.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:28.920
What do you think
sets the offering

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.309
that GKE has today in terms of
what customers are asking for?

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:35.100
APARNA SINHA: Yeah.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:35.640
Sure, yeah.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.460
I've been with the project
for the last 3 and 1/2 years.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.230
So really seeing
it grow from a baby

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.160
until I would say much
broader enterprise

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:50.310
adoption both in the open
source as well as, of course,

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:55.590
on our cloud with GKE and really
have been with the GKE service

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.090
from the beginnings
as well as we

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.300
worked through first
introducing it as a service

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.180
and building all of these
enterprise grade features

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.980
into it for
enterprise readiness,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.230
making sure upgrades are much
more automated and low impact

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.930
for the customers, and
moving into the future,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.550
providing GKE On-Prem.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:17.400
So what I've heard from
customers and users,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:19.260
both open source
users and customers,

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:21.870
is that they want
to be able to use

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.750
GKE in their own on-prem
environment, which is why we're

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.890
introducing GKE On-Prem and why
we introduced it at GCP Next

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.480
and, we're moving
forward with that.

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:35.465
And they want to have Google's
expertise from having managed

00:01:35.465 --> 00:01:39.810
it in production as a service
for the last 3 and 1/2 years

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.340
as well as the
expertise that comes

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:44.430
from being directly involved
deeply with the open source

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:45.100
projects.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.700
So you probably
saw in my keynote,

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.470
I showed the distribution
of contributions

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.200
to Kubernetes open
source over the last year

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.900
based on the CNCS statistics.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:58.180
Google is contributing
40-plus percent of that.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.110
And so what that means is that
we're very current with all

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.430
of the issues with Kubernetes.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:04.590
We're building solutions.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.090
And so when you
partner with Google,

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.870
you have that partnership
with the core maintainers.

00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:12.240
And so, for example,
on GKE, we're

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:18.060
always ahead in knowing
what's coming up for our users

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.790
both in the open source
and on the cloud.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.920
With the security
vulnerabilities

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.400
that are happening, we're always
patching them ahead of time

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:28.650
and then bringing them
into GKE, making sure

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.490
that the most secure
version is there.

00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:34.080
Same thing with the versions and
upgrades finding the problems

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.632
and making sure
that the customers

00:02:36.632 --> 00:02:37.840
don't have to worry about it.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.960
So that's the second piece.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.180
Customers love
Kubernetes I would

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:46.560
say for all of the feature
functionality, the resiliency,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.780
the productivity, the
higher utilization

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.020
that you get when you run your
applications on Kubernetes.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:56.760
But they also don't always want
to manage Kubernetes and have

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.220
to keep up with all of the
capabilities and program

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.330
all of the capabilities.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.960
So we try and provide a much
more managed distribution.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.340
We'll be providing it on prem.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:08.040
We've been providing it in
the cloud for several years,

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:09.600
not only managing your masters--

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.530
obviously managing the sizing
and the backup of your FCD

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.720
and scaling of the masters--

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.720
but also managing much
more of your cluster

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.690
itself with node
upgrades and node auto

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:23.395
repair and node auto scaling.

00:03:23.395 --> 00:03:25.520
These are some of the
features that over time we've

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:28.290
brought in to make sure that
customers can worry less

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.890
about the infrastructure.

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:32.250
And then I think I talked
about in my keynote today.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.170
We're building
also up the stack,

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:38.020
providing now node
auto provisioning.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.390
So you can be hands free.

00:03:40.390 --> 00:03:43.320
You don't have to worry as much
about is the cluster running.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.620
You can focus on what do you
want to run in the cluster.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.370
And so we've actually just
this week in fact earlier today

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:53.200
announced that Istio on
GKE is moving to beta,

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.200
and it's available for
existing clusters as well

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.030
as new clusters.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.340
And we're going to be providing
it with a single click

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.050
install, which I
demoed in the keynote

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:04.380
and also providing basic
lifecycle operations so

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:05.410
upgrading SGO.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:08.640
So it's really a lot
easier to use Istio.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.250
You don't have to know a lot
of the guts of it yourself.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.750
But you can get the
benefit of being

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:16.320
able to monitor and view and
discover all of your services

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.930
across your clusters
and then also be

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:22.800
able to set security policies,
ensure encrypted traffic

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.500
and authentication
and then also be

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.170
able to use the basic
functionalities of a proxy

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:31.830
like for load balancing
obviously and also for traffic

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:32.680
control.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:34.350
So those are the
capabilities you

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:39.120
get for not entirely for free
but much more easily in GKE.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.530
And then lastly is bringing
that developer experience

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.150
with Knative and
the GKE serverless

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.430
add on, which is still
an alpha, but that

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:50.890
is also something customers
have been looking for.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.590
They love Kubernetes,
but then they

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:55.110
want to give their developers
a hands free experience where

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.870
they can just go use all
of the functionality,

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.750
write their code,
focus on their code,

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:01.829
and not have to worry
about all the rest,

00:05:01.829 --> 00:05:03.620
not have to worry about
setting up ingress,

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.290
not have to worry about
exposing their services

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:08.960
and how to expose the services,
not have to worry about how

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.070
to auto scale.

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.040
And those are the things
that Knative does really well

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:14.090
under the covers.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.220
So I would say those
are the three things

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:18.050
that customers been asking for.

00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.619
Give me something in
my own environment,

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:22.910
give me something that I
don't have to manage a lot of,

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:25.835
and give me a good developer
experience for my developers.

00:05:25.835 --> 00:05:30.020
SAAD ALI: So is it fair to
summarize it as with GKE, we've

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:33.880
built this massive open source
ecosystem with Kubernetes,

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:38.810
Istio, Knative, and we've now
made it very, very dead simple

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:40.250
to use on the cloud
side and we're

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:44.690
bringing that simplicity
over to people's data centers

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:48.380
and completing the
story, and is that

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:49.955
what customers are asking for?

00:05:49.955 --> 00:05:52.580
APARNA SINHA: Yeah, well, so I
think the ecosystem is actually

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.077
one of the best parts of
working on this project,

00:05:55.077 --> 00:05:57.410
and one of the best parts of
working with an open source

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:59.210
community.

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.070
The way that the
project is architected,

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:03.470
it's very much
amenable to extensions.

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:04.220
And you know that.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.740
You've been working on
CSI, which just moved to GA

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:09.210
and 1.13, so congratulations.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:10.760
We got a lot of such interfaces.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.430
And what that does is it
really opens the project up

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:15.680
to an ecosystem, a very
rich ecosystem that

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.030
can build and not just build
but also customize Kubernetes

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:22.220
for specific
environments, customize it

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:23.969
for the on-prem
environment that has

00:06:23.969 --> 00:06:26.510
a particular type of storage or
particular type of networking

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.790
preference or particular
type of security preference.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:30.990
So all of those
things are plugable.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.560
And from a business
perspective, what it means

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:37.340
is that we can bring that
ecosystem into our cloud.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:39.360
So Kubernetes, I
tried to think of it--

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.220
I like to think of it and call
it a Kubernetes First Cloud.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.920
Google is a Kubernetes
first cloud.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.370
It is because GKE is one of
the most popular services

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:50.270
on our cloud.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.920
The vast majority
of our customers

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.080
use GKE in some form or another.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:58.190
Everyone from "New York
Times," Paypal, eBay,

00:06:58.190 --> 00:06:59.535
these are some of the--

00:06:59.535 --> 00:07:04.347
and every major retailer,
that's fresh in our minds

00:07:04.347 --> 00:07:06.680
because we just went through
Black Friday, Cyber Monday,

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.650
and we scaled all these
retailers to new heights

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:12.384
in terms of their business,
all with zero outages.

00:07:12.384 --> 00:07:13.550
So very, very proud of that.

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:16.380
Really coming to maturity.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:20.380
So Google is really a
Kubernetes First Cloud.

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:22.670
We think about well how
can Kubernetes run well?

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:24.380
What kind of networking
features can we

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:26.150
build like network
end point groups

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:28.160
as an example, IP
aliases as an example.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:31.010
What can we do to make
the experience better

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:34.280
for Kubernetes users
on Google Cloud.

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:38.420
And when you have the
Kubernetes First Cloud,

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:40.880
I think it makes sense
as an ecosystem provider

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:44.689
to really come in and provide
your offering on Google Cloud.

00:07:44.689 --> 00:07:46.730
And so that's what we've
done with the Kubernetes

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:47.760
marketplace.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:51.230
But then the rich thing
here, the really rich aspect

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:54.710
is that we're providing that GKE
experience now in your own data

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:55.670
center on prem.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:59.600
So that same ecosystem that's in
the Kubernetes marketplace now

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.670
carries over and
now has much broader

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:04.995
reach even beyond
Google Cloud to on prem.

00:08:04.995 --> 00:08:06.830
SAAD ALI: That sounds awesome.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.280
Let's switch gears a little
bit and talk about KubeCon.

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.060
You have been to
quite a few KubeCons.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:12.800
How does this feel
different from others

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:13.370
that you've been to?

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:14.660
APARNA SINHA: I love KubeCon.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:17.340
KubeCon has a real
special place in my heart.

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:20.360
And this is my fifth
or sixth KubeCon.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:23.420
And I've been comparing
and contrasting.

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:25.370
Seattle is of course a
very special location.

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:28.430
We have a big
presence in Seattle.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:32.669
A lot of my team-- a lot of the
Kubernetes team is based here.

00:08:32.669 --> 00:08:33.574
And I've been here--

00:08:33.574 --> 00:08:35.240
I think this is the
second or third time

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:36.470
that we've been in Seattle.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.600
And this is the obviously
the largest KubeCon

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:41.242
that we've had with
8,000 or 9,000 attendees.

00:08:41.242 --> 00:08:42.200
SAAD ALI: 8,000, right.

00:08:42.200 --> 00:08:43.866
APARNA SINHA: And
this is the first time

00:08:43.866 --> 00:08:45.200
we've been in this huge venue.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:47.510
I think it's been
very well done.

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:48.840
It seems very approachable.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.270
A lot of people,
a lot of familiar

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:52.290
faces, the whole
community is here.

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:54.910
So it's really great
to meet everyone.

00:08:54.910 --> 00:08:55.880
Yeah.

00:08:55.880 --> 00:08:56.850
It's a real tradition.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.970
And I think in particular
the Google team

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:00.210
is out here in full force.

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:01.940
I think they got
more than 300 people.

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.140
SAAD ALI: Absolutely.

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:04.515
What are you
looking most forward

00:09:04.515 --> 00:09:07.115
to this week in terms of
activities going on at KubeCon?

00:09:07.115 --> 00:09:09.830
APARNA SINHA: Well, I'm really
looking forward to the keynotes

00:09:09.830 --> 00:09:12.190
by Brian Grant and Tim
Hockin and Clayton.

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.659
SAAD ALI: The stories
of the old days.

00:09:14.659 --> 00:09:15.450
APARNA SINHA: Yeah.

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:15.949
Yeah.

00:09:15.949 --> 00:09:17.900
Yeah, I don't know what
the content will be,

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.340
but that is something that
I'm really looking forward to.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:24.980
And then the Sig meet ups tend
to be-- the Sig meetings tend

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:26.520
to be very popular as well.

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:30.401
There's a diversity
luncheon I believe,

00:09:30.401 --> 00:09:32.900
if it hasn't already passed,
so I'm looking forward to that.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:35.000
And then just
catching up with all

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.600
of the familiar faces in the
community and meeting folks

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:42.740
from other companies, especially
all of these companies

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:46.370
on security and networking,
the whole ecosystem that

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:48.770
has sprung up, I
always really enjoy

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:51.290
meeting them and
hearing from them.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.415
And then, of course,
there are a lot of users.

00:09:53.415 --> 00:09:55.250
There are a lot of users
and customers here,

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:56.570
so that's always the best part.

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:59.160
SAAD ALI: And to
wrap it up, put you

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:02.220
on the spot, what's your
favorite KubeCon memory?

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:05.370
APARNA SINHA: Oh, well, I
don't know if it's my-- yeah,

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:07.240
I guess it is my favorite.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:11.180
So two years ago in 2016
when we came to Seattle,

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:15.250
that was on November 7th
I think or November 6th,

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:17.470
and it was election day.

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:21.670
And the KubeCon after
party on November 6th,

00:10:21.670 --> 00:10:25.150
we were all in this
like wonderful venue,

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:27.430
all kinds of candles
and beautiful lights

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:30.100
and great snacks and everybody
was watching the election

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:32.560
and everybody was expecting
it to go a certain way

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.150
and it went a different way.

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:36.850
And then the morning
after just being

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.580
with-- it's kind of like being
with your family a little bit,

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:41.750
but it's a much more
extended family.

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:46.360
And I felt like it was nice
being with the cloud native

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:46.870
community.

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:48.850
SAAD ALI: Yeah, I was
talking to Tim earlier today,

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:50.470
and he says even
though this is scaled

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:54.020
to the scale of 8,000 people, it
still feels like a big family.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:56.050
And I think that's
the beauty of KubeCon.

00:10:56.050 --> 00:10:58.270
So thank you very much
for your time, Aparna.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.270
APARNA SINHA: Thank you.

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:00.580
SAAD ALI: We're
going to hand it off.

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:02.288
APARNA SINHA: Yeah,
great talking to you.

00:11:02.288 --> 00:11:04.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

